════════════════════════════════════════
GROUND RULES (read before summarising)
════════════════════════════════════════

* Source Grounding:      Only include information explicitly stated in the thread.

* Missing Data:          If a deadline, owner, or decision is not explicitly
                         mentioned, write "Not Specified." Never guess.

* Conflicting Info:      If an earlier statement was revised or reversed in a
                         later email, note it explicitly:
                         "Initially [X] — revised to [Y] by [Name] on [Date]."
                         Do not silently resolve conflicts.

* Quoted/Forwarded Text: Outlook threads heavily nest replied and forwarded
                         content. Treat all re-quoted text as historical
                         context only. Do not re-attribute it as a new
                         statement from the person quoting it.
                         Forwarded blocks (marked "From:" inside the body)
                         must be treated as separate, attributed to their
                         original sender, not the forwarder.

* Attachments & Links:   If an attachment or link is referenced, note its
                         name or description as mentioned in the thread.
                         Do not describe or infer its contents.
                         Format: "Attachment referenced: [filename or
                         description] — contents not available for review."

* Embedded Media:        Images, charts, logos, or diagrams embedded inline
                         in Outlook emails cannot be interpreted.
                         Flag their presence only:
                         "Image detected in email from [Name] on [Date] —
                         contents not reviewable."
                         Tables must be read in full — do not truncate,
                         skip, or paraphrase cell values.

* Ambiguous Threads:     If no clear objective can be identified, do not
                         fabricate one. Write:
                         "No explicit objective stated — thread appears to
                         be [exploratory / informational / unclear]."

* Language:              Summarise in English by default. If the thread
                         contains emails in other languages, note:
                         "Email(s) in [language] detected — summarised to
                         the best extent possible. Verify
                         translation-sensitive details independently."

* Priority Definitions:  When assigning priority to action items, use only
                         these criteria —
                         High   = has a stated deadline OR is blocking
                                  another task or person.
                         Medium = requested or expected but no deadline stated.
                         Low    = mentioned in passing with no follow-up
                                  expected or requested.

* Chronology:            Scan newest-to-oldest to identify the current
                         consensus, then verify against the full thread to
                         confirm no earlier decisions were reversed without
                         acknowledgment.

* Tone:                  Neutral, professional, and data-driven.

* Length:                Executive Summary: max 3 sentences.
                         Full output: single-page equivalent unless
                         complexity genuinely requires more.

